{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3","id":432851,"title":"Reasons for Great Maelstoms 1..2..3..","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-08T11:44:53-07:00","id":432851,"title":"Reasons for Great Maelstoms 1..2..3..","content":"I imagine if you know about Wraith the you will know about the Five Great Maelstroms which caused devastation to Stygia in particular.<br /> Looking at them not all would seem to cause a cataclysm on a world (or underworld) changing level.<br /> For instance I can see why the fall of Rome would cause the First Great Maelstrom in Europe, but not in the Kingdoms of Jade or Ivory to name a few.<br /> I also realise that written from a western-centric point of view in the beginning of WtO, the Great Maelstroms as they are also make perfect sense.<br /> <br /> What has gained my curiosity is, as GM's how would you portray these events from a non-stygian viewpoint?<br /> <br /> Do other levels of the underworld experience other disasters which could be heralded as Great Maelstroms, or are the first ones recorded the ones shown as one of the Five?<br /> <br /> I imagine that the fall of The Flayed Lands might seem all the more traumatic for the inhabitants of that Kingdom if the Heretics were shown to have the power to raise an evil such as the Maelstrom, a previously unknown phenomenon. Or do they simply view it as something which heralds the next cycle, a broom to sweep away the old to prepare for the cycle to come.<br /> <br /> Was the arrival of the Jade Emperor preceded or announced with a Great Maelstrom?<br /> <br /> I'd like to know what people think, the source material is so much fun I can't help my let my imagination run riot <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>I'm God's hated messenger, the Devil's cohort<br /> I'm the one they all blame, when a life is cut short<br /> But still if you're willing, to give it a try<br /> I'll beat you at anything, and claim you're last sigh</i><br /> - The Reaper's Tales","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4688-malkira","name":"Malkira","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-08T12:29:13-07:00","id":432939,"title":"","content":"In general, the arrival of a Great Maelstrom is that it reflects the arrival of a huge increase in entropy into the world - civilizational collapse, mass death, societal despair, etc. Whether the Maelstrom is the final result of an ongoing build up of such things, or whether it is also a harbinger of things to come is debateable. In any event, it is a signal to all Wraiths that &quot;Oblivion has increased its power greatly.&quot;<br /> <br /> It doesn't matter where that occurred. So not all Wraiths or Dark Kingdoms may know of the proximate cause; nevertheless they know something bad happened. It may not even be what people think it is.<br /> <br /> Going back to the First Great Maelstrom, there is actually a lot of problems with the official story (this is most likely due to the ignorance of real history by the writers). The official story is that Rome fell so the First Great Maelstrom was caused as a result. This actually does not make any sense.<br /> <br /> 1) The Roman Empire did not actually fall because the Eastern Roman Empire still survived and would continue to exist for another thousand years. In fact, it was the more populated and rich half, and in the next hundred years would ever reclaim significant portions of the western empire.<br /> <br /> 2) No great traumatic event occurred in the Skinlands in AD 476. All that happened is that an officer of the Roman army (Odoacer) deposed his commander (Orestes) and sent the titular emperor of the western empire (Romulus Augustulus) into a monastery and sent the imperial regalia back to Constantinople. Rather than appoint a new puppet empire himself, Odoacer simply asked the emperor in Constantinople to recognize his Roman military title and as Germanic king, which the emperor did.<br /> <br /> 3) Rome was not even the capital of the western empire at the time; Ravenna was. Furthermore, it was not sacked in AD 476. When Rome was actually sacked in AD 455, there was no maelstrom.<br /> <br /> 4) The actual damage to the empire had already been done by that point, and the west was actually in a stable situation again - albeit with non-Romans ruling most of the empire. The real devastation was to come later with Justinian's attempt to reconquer the west during the Gothic Wars, and with the collapse of the Mediterranean economy when the Muslims invaded a century later.<br /> <br /> So from the actual perspective of wraiths in AD 476, the First Great Maelstrom likely did not make any sense. There either needed to be some kind of secret history of why it happened, or somehow Fate knew that this was actually a turning point in human history that would not be apparent to most people at the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-08T13:49:22-07:00","id":433079,"title":"","content":"While not a single event, something that relates to the down fall of the Roman Empire and a major changes in the metaphysical balance of power in the cWOD is the fall of the White Howlers (wiped out ~200 BCE) and the emergence of the Black Spiral Dancers are a full Tribe following the Wyrm (contradictory information in the books on how long it took for them to truly become fully dedicated to the Wyrm).<br /> <br /> Wraiths wouldn't know the specifics of that, but probably assigned a period of increasing metaphysical entropy that finally built into the First Great Maelstrom the sacking of Rome more as a symbolic marker more than belief that the sacking itself set it off fully on its own.<br /> <br /> There are also significant Shadowlands events that are linked to many of the Great Maelstroms. Such events could be linked to what pushes something into a Great Maelstrom or not (The Third was caused by the conquest of the Americas but also the destruction of the Flayed Lands on top of it).<br /> <br /> Most of the Great Maelstroms are also associated with periods of time, not single events. Even if a single event is seen as the final straw. It also makes sense that the periods seem to shrink. The decline of the Roman Empire took centuries, the plague took decades if you include the Asia parts of the pandemic, the conquest of Mesoamerica also took a bit of time, The two World Wars were only a few years, and the Week of Nightmares was... a week. It makes sense that the First Maelstrom took some time to build up as each seems to grow in power more and more quickly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-08T18:59:05-07:00","id":433414,"title":"","content":"I would imagine that the six Great Malestroms as we know them are only described as such by Stygia, with the Yellow Springs and Swar experiencing their own localized storms at other periods... at least until the Fourth of Fifth, which were appropriately international.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T03:46:25-07:00","id":435524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=432939#post432939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In general, the arrival of a Great Maelstrom is that it reflects the arrival of a huge increase in entropy into the world - civilizational collapse, mass death, societal despair, etc. Whether the Maelstrom is the final result of an ongoing build up of such things, or whether it is also a harbinger of things to come is debateable. In any event, it is a signal to all Wraiths that &quot;Oblivion has increased its power greatly.&quot;<br /> <br /> It doesn't matter where that occurred. So not all Wraiths or Dark Kingdoms may know of the proximate cause; nevertheless they know something bad happened. It may not even be what people think it is.<br /> <br /> Going back to the First Great Maelstrom, there is actually a lot of problems with the official story (this is most likely due to the ignorance of real history by the writers). The official story is that Rome fell so the First Great Maelstrom was caused as a result. This actually does not make any sense.<br /> <br /> 1) The Roman Empire did not actually fall because the Eastern Roman Empire still survived and would continue to exist for another thousand years. In fact, it was the more populated and rich half, and in the next hundred years would ever reclaim significant portions of the western empire.<br /> <br /> 2) No great traumatic event occurred in the Skinlands in AD 476. All that happened is that an officer of the Roman army (Odoacer) deposed his commander (Orestes) and sent the titular emperor of the western empire (Romulus Augustulus) into a monastery and sent the imperial regalia back to Constantinople. Rather than appoint a new puppet empire himself, Odoacer simply asked the emperor in Constantinople to recognize his Roman military title and as Germanic king, which the emperor did.<br /> <br /> 3) Rome was not even the capital of the western empire at the time; Ravenna was. Furthermore, it was not sacked in AD 476. When Rome was actually sacked in AD 455, there was no maelstrom.<br /> <br /> 4) The actual damage to the empire had already been done by that point, and the west was actually in a stable situation again - albeit with non-Romans ruling most of the empire. The real devastation was to come later with Justinian's attempt to reconquer the west during the Gothic Wars, and with the collapse of the Mediterranean economy when the Muslims invaded a century later.<br /> <br /> So from the actual perspective of wraiths in AD 476, the First Great Maelstrom likely did not make any sense. There either needed to be some kind of secret history of why it happened, or somehow Fate knew that this was actually a turning point in human history that would not be apparent to most people at the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I could probably take this a couple ways...<br /> <br /> 1) The sacking of Rome 21 years prior was still the defining event of GM1, it just took a long time for that particular bubble to burst. The wound left by GM1 allowed the later ones to come more quickly after their defining event.<br /> <br /> 2) Conspiracy! The sacking of Rome was actually a scapegoat explanation for what actually happened... Maybe Charon got too big for his britches and thought he could put an end to oblivion with an army of soldiers recently conscripted from the sacking of Rome, or some incredibly powerful relic, like the fruit from the tree of knowledge, or something like that. Something like what Xerxes Jones attempted at the end of the wraith line.... and, kablooey.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T06:03:47-07:00","id":435571,"title":"","content":"It could be, of course, that something happened outside the Dark Kingdom of Iron that caused the First Great Maelstrom, and the Western-centric viewpoint of the Stygian wraiths made them improperly attribute it to something Roman. For example, Wikipedia lists a number of events: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/476\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/476</a>.<br /> <br /> Perhaps it was actually this: <b>China<span style=\"color:#555555\">[</span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=476&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" target=\"_blank\">edit</a><span style=\"color:#555555\">]</span></b><br /><br /><ul><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Xianwen_of_Northern_Wei\" target=\"_blank\">Xian Wen Di</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taishang_Huang\" target=\"_blank\">Retired Emperor</a> of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Wei\" target=\"_blank\">Northern Wei</a>, is murdered by Empress <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empress_Feng_(Wencheng)\" target=\"_blank\">Feng</a>. She assumes <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regent\" target=\"_blank\">regency</a> over the young <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Xiaowen_of_Northern_Wei\" target=\"_blank\">Xiao Wen Di</a>.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T10:24:32-07:00","id":435892,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=433414#post433414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would imagine that the six Great Malestroms as we know them are only described as such by Stygia, with the Yellow Springs and Swar experiencing their own localized storms at other periods... at least until the Fourth of Fifth, which were appropriately international.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I'm standing with this. There's no reason to think that Stygia now or ever has had an ounce of cultural relativism. All that's probably important to that scheme of numbering Maelstroms is whether they happened to Stygia. The later ones occur on a more global stage so they are actually Panglobal Maelstroms, but it seems odd to comb through world history to find things that happened at exactly the same time as the fall of Rome to explain a Maelstrom happening in China, North America, etc., particularly when the events just don't seem to add up to the kind of catastrophic change, either cumulative or instantaneous . <br /> <br /> Maybe the last Great Maelstrom in North American before the annihilation of the Native Americans by the arrival of Europeans was the fall of Cahokia? Before that, perhaps the Hypsithermal? Perhaps there was a lesser and more localized, but still great Maelstrom at the end of the Anasazi civilization? And those don't track with the Black Death, etc. <br /> <br /> China definitely has flashpoints of it's own, but, as far as seminal moments of change in history, the Qin Empire seems like it would have generated a Maelstrom from the shear amount of Pathos and how MUCH was coming across the Shroud. Oh, and ended up producing the eternal (until suborned and replaced) Emperor of the Dark Kingdom of Jade. But it doesn't match up to any of the &quot;Great Maelstroms&quot; of Stygia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T09:59:59-07:00","id":437148,"title":"","content":"The Mongols under Genghis Khan and his heirs killed almost as many people as the Black Death and overthrew societies throughout Asia, the Middle East, and Eastern Europe. It seems odd to me that there <i>wasn't</i> a Great Maelstrom in the 13th century.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T10:50:25-07:00","id":437226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=435892#post435892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think I'm standing with this. There's no reason to think that Stygia now or ever has had an ounce of cultural relativism. All that's probably important to that scheme of numbering Maelstroms is whether they happened to Stygia. The later ones occur on a more global stage so they are actually Panglobal Maelstroms, but it seems odd to comb through world history to find things that happened at exactly the same time as the fall of Rome to explain a Maelstrom happening in China, North America, etc., particularly when the events just don't seem to add up to the kind of catastrophic change, either cumulative or instantaneous .<br /> <br /> Maybe the last Great Maelstrom in North American before the annihilation of the Native Americans by the arrival of Europeans was the fall of Cahokia? Before that, perhaps the Hypsithermal? Perhaps there was a lesser and more localized, but still great Maelstrom at the end of the Anasazi civilization? And those don't track with the Black Death, etc.<br /> <br /> China definitely has flashpoints of it's own, but, as far as seminal moments of change in history, the Qin Empire seems like it would have generated a Maelstrom from the shear amount of Pathos and how MUCH was coming across the Shroud. Oh, and ended up producing the eternal (until suborned and replaced) Emperor of the Dark Kingdom of Jade. But it doesn't match up to any of the &quot;Great Maelstroms&quot; of Stygia.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. I think I read that the Yellow Springs doesn't experience the Great Maelstroms in the same way that Stygia does, anyway. They probably wouldn't count the GMs in the same way as Stygia. It makes sense that what is a full-blown GM in the West might just be a ripple (well, a slight underexaggeration, but you know what I mean) in the Yellow Springs, and vice versa.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T18:39:14-07:00","id":437838,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=437148#post437148\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Mongols under Genghis Khan and his heirs killed almost as many people as the Black Death and overthrew societies throughout Asia, the Middle East, and Eastern Europe. It seems odd to me that there <i>wasn't</i> a Great Maelstrom in the 13th century.</div> </div> </div> </div>I concur. A stack of over 1 million skulls left in Baghdad... That's ONE city (albeit a large one).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T05:40:46-07:00","id":438297,"title":"","content":"RealDeadGuy did draw a parallel between Adonai and Noah (i.e., The Flood) and the Great Maelstroms/Grandma. If that were an indication, the GMs might be a punishment by Yahweh upon the wraiths of the West. As the Jade Emperor rules over the East (arguably another aspect of the same god, but one with a different covenant with his people), it might only be Western misdeeds that are punished this way? Yomi Wan might be the August Personage's preferred method of punishing the wayward souls of the East.<br /> <br /> This is clutching at straws, but we're often presented with the possibility that West and East are separated cosmologically because of regional paradigms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-14T21:55:01-07:00","id":442180,"title":"","content":"The Book of Oblivion will cover this stuff in more detail.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T08:07:01-07:00","id":442553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=438297#post438297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RealDeadGuy did draw a parallel between Adonai and Noah (i.e., The Flood) and the Great Maelstroms/Grandma. If that were an indication, the GMs might be a punishment by Yahweh upon the wraiths of the West. As the Jade Emperor rules over the East (arguably another aspect of the same god, but one with a different covenant with his people), it might only be Western misdeeds that are punished this way? Yomi Wan might be the August Personage's preferred method of punishing the wayward souls of the East.<br /> <br /> This is clutching at straws, but we're often presented with the possibility that West and East are separated cosmologically because of regional paradigms.</div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;West&quot; and &quot;East&quot; are no more different and, in some ways much LESS different than many other regions on the globe. If regional paradigms are the rule, we should be seeing A LOT of different regimes with different Maelstrom thresholds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T09:50:01-07:00","id":442705,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/432851-reasons-for-great-maelstoms-1-2-3?p=435524#post435524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I could probably take this a couple ways...<br /> <br /> 1) The sacking of Rome 21 years prior was still the defining event of GM1, it just took a long time for that particular bubble to burst. The wound left by GM1 allowed the later ones to come more quickly after their defining event.<br /> <br /> 2) Conspiracy! The sacking of Rome was actually a scapegoat explanation for what actually happened... Maybe Charon got too big for his britches and thought he could put an end to oblivion with an army of soldiers recently conscripted from the sacking of Rome, or some incredibly powerful relic, like the fruit from the tree of knowledge, or something like that. Something like what Xerxes Jones attempted at the end of the wraith line.... and, kablooey.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ends of Empire offers a secret history for the First GM - the Tithe of the Dead. Charon demanded a tithe from new wraiths, and if they couldn't pay by the classic coins, they paid with their eyes or their hands. A *lot* paid this way, and many of these maimed and blinded wraiths sought redress from Oblivion... which eventually answered with the Great Maelstrom.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":51,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","user":"marin","date":". 03-15-2015, 12:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}